Architecture and Principles
***************************

.[perex]
What happens to a text from the moment Texy receives it until the HTML is ready? And how come Markdown and Texy syntax get the same typography, the same HTML fixes and the same tools? This page explains how Texy is built on the inside. You don't need it for everyday use, but it will help you when you extend Texy.


One Tree for All Languages
==========================

Simple converters turn text into HTML through a series of replacements: they find `**`, replace it with `<strong>`, and so on. Texy works differently. It first **parses the text into a document tree**, in which each element, be it a paragraph, a heading or a link, is one object. Only then is the output created from this tree.

The tree doesn't depend on the language the text was written in. Markdown and Texy syntax turn text into the same nodes, and everything that follows works with the tree alone:

```
Markdown ──────┐                                   ┌──> HTML
               ├──> tree ──> transformations ──>───┼──> plain text
Texy syntax ───┘    (typography, security, …)      ├──> Texy syntax
                                                   └──> Markdown
```

That's why we call Texy an engine. Typography, HTML repair, safe mode and renderers are written once, and every language gets them. And converting Markdown to Texy syntax is simply a matter of which language reads the text and which one prints it.

Here is the tree created from the Markdown [article on the home page |@home#The First Article], simplified and without the helper `ContentNode` nodes that hold the content. The same text written in Texy syntax produces the same tree:

```
DocumentNode
  HeadingNode level=1
    TextNode "How We Dried Plums"
  ParagraphNode
    TextNode "This year we picked "
    PhraseNode Strong
      TextNode "over 40 kg"
    TextNode " of plums. What to do with them? Grandma says: …"
  ParagraphNode
    TextNode "We bought the dryer at the "
    LinkNode https://example.com/dryer
      TextNode "shop by the station"
    TextNode " for $60-80."
```

Nodes come in two kinds: **block** nodes, such as a paragraph or a heading, and **inline** nodes, such as text, emphasis or a link inside a paragraph. They all live in the `Texy\Nodes` namespace, and you'll find an overview of them on the [Customizing Elements |custom-handlers#Node Overview] page.


Four Processing Steps
=====================

Calling `$texy->toHtml($text)` or `$markdown->toHtml($text)` runs four steps one after another. You can also call them one by one when you need to get at the tree:

```php
$document = $texy->parse($text);      // steps 1 and 2: normalization and parsing
$texy->transform($document);          // step 3: transformations of the document
$html = $texy->renderHtml($document); // step 4: rendering
```

Markdown has the same three methods, `$markdown->parse()`, `$markdown->transform()` and `$markdown->renderHtml()`. A document can be transformed only once; a second call to `transform()` throws an exception.


Normalization
-------------

`Texy::normalize()` tidies up the text: it converts all line endings to `\n` and removes the BOM, control characters, soft hyphens, trailing spaces, and blank lines at the beginning and the end, keeping just the newline after the last line. Tabs stay, and the syntax counts each as a single space. Markdown has its own, gentler `Markdown::normalize()`, which unifies line endings and removes control characters but keeps trailing spaces, because two of them mean a line break. Text that is not valid UTF-8 makes it throw an exception right away, instead of causing an unexpected error somewhere further down the line.


Parsing
-------

Each syntax of the language, such as bold text or a table, has a name, a regular expression that recognizes it in the text, and a function, the handler, that creates a node from the matched text. Parsing happens on two levels. The block parser goes through the lines and finds blocks: headings, lists, tables. It splits the text between them into paragraphs. The inline parser then finds emphasis, links and images in the content of the blocks.

Nesting works by having the handler parse the inner text again, for example the content of a blockquote as further blocks. A handler's output is never scanned again, so Texy can never mistake something it produced itself for syntax.

When several syntaxes compete for the same text, the one that starts earliest wins. For inline syntaxes with the same start, the longer one wins; with the same length, an application's syntax beats a built-in one, and after that the one registered first wins. For block syntaxes, length doesn't matter: an application's syntax beats a built-in one right away, and after that the one registered first wins. A handler can also reject the matched text, and then the syntaxes after it get their chance. [Adding Custom Syntax |custom-syntax] shows how to use this for your own syntax.

Markdown parses text with the same core, just in two phases: first all the blocks, and only then their inline content, because a link may refer to a definition that appears later in the text. And the proof that the core is well built: Markdown running on it passes all 652 examples of the CommonMark specification.

The result of parsing is the **syntax tree**. It describes the text as written: each node's `$range` holds the range of the syntax it came from, references in Texy syntax are not resolved yet, and headings have the level the author gave them. Markdown resolves references during parsing already, because they decide what counts as a link. The [source text tools |source-tools] work with this tree.


Transforming the Document
-------------------------

`transform()` runs the transformations that give the tree its meaning. The built-in transformations of Texy syntax run in this order:

1. `{{…}}` directives are handled and replaced
2. links and images written as references get their URL from the definition
3. headings are balanced
4. HTML tags written in the text are paired and checked against the allowed tags, and disallowed ones become text
5. headings are shifted according to `headingTop` and get an `id`

Markdown has only the last two steps, which both languages share, plus a filter for dangerous tags before them in GFM mode.

The order is deliberate. Directives come first because they can change the settings the rest of the document is processed with. The HTML check has to run before typography, because a disallowed tag becomes text that needs to be typeset. And headings get their `id` before your passes read them.

Then [your passes |custom-handlers#Modifying the Document] run, and finally **typography**: quotes, dashes, non-breaking spaces and hyphenation of long words. Typography works not on the finished HTML but on the text of the nodes in the tree. That's why every output gets it and it never touches a code block.

After the transformations, the tree is a **document**: it describes what the text means and contains everything the output needs.

Security belongs to the transformations for the same reason. This is where it is decided which HTML tags are allowed, and only what is allowed stays in the tree. So it applies to every output and to both languages: Markdown parses its raw HTML into the same tag nodes as Texy syntax, and they go through the same check.


Rendering
---------

A renderer walks through the document and produces the output for each node. For HTML this is `Texy\Output\Html\Renderer`, which follows the `$texy->htmlOutput` settings. At the end it checks the HTML: it closes unclosed tags, fixes improperly nested ones, and drops a tag that doesn't belong in a given place, keeping only its content. It indents and wraps the result so that it is readable.

Rendering doesn't change the tree. You can render the same document as many times as you like, and into different outputs:

```php
$html = $texy->renderHtml($document);   // HTML
$text = $texy->renderText($document);   // plain text, e.g. for email or search
```

Markdown has no `renderText()` method; you get plain text from its document with `(new Texy\Output\Text\Renderer)->render($document)`.

A node with no renderer for HTML or plain text is rendered simply as its content. So a document containing a node known only to one extension can always be rendered into these outputs. The printer that turns a document back into source text, however, rejects such a node.


Hooking into the Processing
===========================

Each step has its own extension point:

- **a new syntax** for parsing: `addInlineSyntax()` and `addBlockSyntax()`, see [Adding Custom Syntax |custom-syntax]
- **modifying the document**: `addPass()` and directives, see [Customizing Elements |custom-handlers#Modifying the Document]
- **different rendering**: `$texy->htmlOutput->addRenderer()`, see [Customizing Elements |custom-handlers#Different HTML for an Element]
- **working with the source text**: the syntax tree from `parse()`, see [Source Text Tools |source-tools]

A simple question tells you where a change belongs: does it change the content of the document regardless of the output? Then it belongs in the transformations, and every output gets it. Does it change only how one output looks? Then it belongs in the renderer. The [settings |configuration] are split the same way: what changes the meaning of the text is in `$texy->config`, what changes only how the HTML looks is in `$texy->htmlOutput`.


Glossary
========

A **node** is one element of the document tree, an object from `Texy\Nodes`.

A **syntax** is a named construct of the language, such as `phrase/strong` or `image`. Its name is used to turn it on and off in the `allowedSyntax` array.

A **pattern** is the regular expression by which a syntax is recognized in the text.

A **syntax handler** creates a node from the matched text, or rejects the matched text.

A **pass** is a function that receives the whole document and may modify it.

A **renderer** produces the output for a single node.

A **range** (`Texy\Range`) tells you which part of the source text a node came from.

